 `keystone-testkit` depends on common/bean/item/persistence, so Maven forbids those four from consuming it even at test scope — they keep their own local fixtures. `keystone-command`, `keystone-hooks`, `keystone-module`, `keystone-npc`, `keystone-plugin` and external consumers may use it. **Never add it (or Mockito) to `keystone-plugin`'s shade** — audit with `unzip -l keystone-plugin/target/Keystone-<version>.jar | grep -i "testkit\|mockito"` (must be empty).
 
 ## Hard design rules
 
-- **Layering:** Keystone holds only low-level, generic infrastructure. If a class knows about gangs, menus, weapons, or any product concept, it does not belong here. **Oriel owns inventory/menu code permanently** — none of it ever goes into Keystone. Scoreboard/hologram code stays in Gangland. `keystone-npc` is generic NPC-driving infrastructure only (navigation, combat cadence, difficulty, entity spawning, mark persistence); product concepts riding on top of it — wanted levels, civilian/police marks, trader shops, what fires a ranged attack — stay in the consumer, reached through `keystone-npc`'s SPI (`NpcRangedAttack`, `NpcTargetFilter`, `NpcMarkDefaults`), never hardcoded here.
+- **Layering:** Keystone holds only low-level, generic infrastructure. If a class knows about gangs, menus, weapons, or any product concept, it does not belong here. **Oriel owns inventory/menu code permanently** — none of it ever goes into Keystone. Scoreboard/hologram code stays in Gangland. `keystone-npc` is generic NPC-driving infrastructure only (navigation, combat cadence, difficulty, entity spawning, mark persistence); product concepts riding on top of it — wanted levels, civilian/police marks, trader shops, what fires a ranged attack — stay in the consumer, reached through `keystone-npc`'s SPI (`NpcRangedAttack`, `NpcTargetFilter`, `NpcMarkDefaults`), never hardcoded here. As of 1.10.0 (phase H10), hologram code lives in `keystone-hologram` and the headless shop transaction/valuation system lives in `keystone-shop` — both generic enough to belong here, superseding the "hologram" and "trader shops" exclusions above for those two systems specifically, while UI-coupled shop admin views and scoreboard code still stay in the consumer (see `docs/keystone-hologram.md` / `docs/keystone-shop.md`).
 - **API floor: Spigot 1.16.5** (`api-version: 1.16`). Never reference newer Bukkit API from Keystone code; version-specific behaviour branches on `NmsVersion`/`NmsCache` (reflection-first, no per-version source modules).
 - **Java 17 only** — no Java 21 language features (Oriel's toolchain is 17).
 - **Shared-classloader constraint:** every consumer plugin sees the same loaded Keystone classes, so never add a static singleton holding per-plugin data (registries, caches, `shared()` accessors). Each consumer instantiates its own `BeanFactory`, `FileManager`, `DatabaseManager`, `Diagnostics` install. Static caches keyed on server-global facts (e.g. `NmsCache`) are fine. Keep public APIs backward compatible within a major version — a Keystone upgrade hits every consumer at once.
 - **Hard third-party dependencies only in `keystone-hooks` and `keystone-npc`** (Vault, PlaceholderAPI, both `provided`; `keystone-npc` adds Citizens, `provided`, reached exclusively through `NpcSupport`, which degrades cleanly when Citizens is absent). Everything else is Bukkit + JDK + the shaded libraries.
 - **Shaded libraries (HikariCP, XSeries, exp4j, commodore) are deliberately NOT relocated** — Keystone is the only plugin shipping them and consumers use them through Keystone's classpath. Don't "fix" this.
