+# keystone-shop
+
+[Back to Documentation Index](./README.md)
+
+Headless shop system, promoted from Gangland Warfare's `gangland-ui/shop-api` in 1.10.0 (phase H10, the decoupling
+wave — `docs/phase-h10-hologram-shop.md`): shop definitions, buy/sell/barter transaction services, category-based
+valuation and YAML persistence. A **zero-DI pure library** — no `keystone-bean` dependency, no listeners, no
+commands. Generic enough to reuse in a non-Gangland consumer.
+
+Depends on `keystone-item` (item builder/serializer/refresher registries), `keystone-persistence` (`FileHandler`/
+`FileManager`/`FolderLoader`, config parsing), `keystone-common`, `XSeries`. Shaded into `Keystone.jar` by
+`keystone-plugin`.
+
+## What stayed behind
+
+Two files that would otherwise have moved stay in the consumer, on purpose:
+
+- `ShopUiSettings` — an interface only the *core admin UI* implements/consumes; shipping it from a headless module
+  would mean zero classes inside `keystone-shop` itself ever reference it — the exact product-concept leak the
+  layering rule exists to avoid.
+- `ShopEditPersistenceHandler` — a 12-line `@ListenerHandler` bean that would have made `keystone-shop` the first
+  Keystone module ever to ship an *active* `Listener` (Keystone ships plain `Event` subclasses, e.g. `keystone-npc`'s
+  `NpcEvent`, and this module's own `ShopEditedEvent` — but never something Keystone itself registers). Leaving it
+  in the consumer keeps `keystone-shop` DI-free.
+
+Both boundaries mean `keystone-shop` never needs `keystone-bean`.
+
+## Economy seam
+
+`PaymentHandler` is a plain interface (`java.math.BigDecimal` amounts only) — it never imports Keystone's
+`EconomyHandler` (`keystone-hooks`). A consumer wanting real money adapts its own economy provider to
+`PaymentHandler`, exactly as Gangland's `TraderBuyListener.adapt(EconomyHandler)` does. This keeps the transaction
+services usable by any consumer, Vault-backed or not.
+
+## Display resolution
+
+`ShopDisplayResolver` is a pure interface; `DefaultShopDisplayResolver` (renamed from Gangland's
+`GanglandShopDisplayResolver`) is the shipped default — resolves an item's clean display name from the live
+stack first, falls back to a pristine rebuild via `ItemDefinitions.pristine`, then to a humanised material name.
+
+## Classes
+
+| Package | Contents |
+|---|---|
+| `org.luckyraven.keystone.shop` | `ShopDefinition`, `ShopItemEntry`, `ShopRegistry` (extends Keystone's `FolderLoader`), `BarterCategory`, `SellCategory`, `EntryKind` |
+| `.event` | `ShopEditedEvent` — plain Bukkit `Event`, fired by the consumer's admin UI |
+| `.io` | `ShopYamlReader`, `ShopYamlWriter` |
+| `.message` | `ShopDisplayResolver`, `ShopMessageContract` (interfaces, no shipped message default), `DefaultShopDisplayResolver` |
+| `.transaction` | `PaymentHandler`, `PaymentException`, `ShopPurchaseService`/`ShopSellService`/`ShopBarterService`, their `*Outcome`/`*Result` types |
+| `.valuation` | `ItemValuation`, `SellValuator`, `CategorySellValuator`, `CategoryBarterValuator` |
+
+## Test fixture note
+
+Several ported tests need Bukkit's `Registry`-backed static resolution (`Material.isAir()`, XSeries lookups) to
+work in a plain unit test. `keystone-testkit`'s `BukkitRegistryFixture` (promoted alongside this module, same
+phase) installs a minimal proxy-backed `Server` for exactly that — call `BukkitRegistryFixture.install()` from
+`@BeforeAll`.
